A||||8||fierojunky||Torque convertor lockup override ||||5||
Z||000000||fierojunky||05-29-2001||09:15 AM||||does anyone know if you could turn this off when you need that little bit of extra rpm? Maybe rig a switch like the older 700R4's. <BR>Sometimes it willn't downshift but If I could unlock the convertor it would probably give it the umph it needs.  I know the brake pedal trick - but unless i stay on the brake it keeps locking back up.  LMK.  Thanks ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||05-29-2001||09:57 AM||||Since you can disarm it by simply unplugging the electrical connector I suppose you could wire in a switch to disarm it from the driver's seat.  There are only two wires to the solenoid so I guess you could wire a switch in either one.  I'm not sure how much current it draws to know whether you could directly wire in a switch or have to use a relay.  Maybe Ogre could answer this one.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||05-29-2001||10:58 AM||||The ECM controls the TCC by closing the ground to the TCC. I was thinking of putting a switch on the TCC myself since at certian RPM and speeds in traffic the TCC is constantly engaging and disengaging and causes me to have to let off or get on the throttle constantly. after 20 minutes of slow traffic my right leg is a bit tired.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/sig.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000003||theogre||05-29-2001||01:30 PM||||<A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/" TARGET=_blank>Transmission Exchange Co.</A> has a TCC delay module for TH125. They claim it helps the above problems. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||05-29-2001||03:55 PM||||If you want to make a simple override switch then put a on/off toggle (or equivalent) in-line between the brake switch and the solenoid.  The wire color is purple and should be traceable from the brake switch.||||reg||3
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-29-2001||03:59 PM||||wouldnt that make your brake lights go on too?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||05-29-2001||10:05 PM||||If your thinking of what Im thinking of, then yes that would turn the brake lights on.||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||05-29-2001||11:25 PM||||Nope, won't turn on the lights since the TCC (via brake switch) is always hot and is activated by the ECM completing the "ground" side of the switch.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>Three parts of the system must work in order for the TCC to engage. <BR>1 Brake circut must be closed. <BR>2 third gear switch must be closed  <BR>3 the ECM will close the ground and engage the solenoid when the speed is constant and above 35 mph.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/sig.jpg">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 05-30-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000008||deceler8||05-30-2001||09:19 AM||||Seems to me that some auto equipped cars have a shift handle with a switch to disable the overdrive...seems if you could find one and get it to fit, you could wire one up that way...would look OE that way.<P>Not sure if any GM cars were equipped this way, though.||||reg||1
